[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering CSS Positioning Schemes in CSS3"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how elements are laid out in a web page is crucial for effective frontend development. The CSS 'position' property is the foundation of layout control, allowing you to manipulate the default flow of documents. This tutorial explores the five core positioning schemes available in CSS3: static, relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Static Positioning"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Static is the default positioning scheme for all HTML elements. An element with 'position: static' is positioned according to the normal flow of the page. Properties like 'top', 'bottom', 'left', 'right', and 'z-index' have no effect on statically positioned elements."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":".element {
  position: static;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Relative Positioning"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A relatively positioned element is positioned relative to its normal position. Setting offset properties ('top', 'right', 'bottom', 'left') will cause the element to adjust its location away from its normal spot, but the space it normally occupied will be preserved in the layout flow."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":".box-relative {
  position: relative;
  top: 20px;
  left: 10px;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Absolute Positioning"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"An absolutely positioned element is removed entirely from the normal document flow. The space it would have occupied is closed up. Its location is determined relative to its nearest positioned ancestor (an ancestor with 'position' set to relative, absolute, or fixed). If no such ancestor exists, it is positioned relative to the initial containing block (usually the viewport)."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":".container {
  position: relative;
}
.box-absolute {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  right: 0;
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Fixed Positioning"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Fixed positioning is a specialized form of absolute positioning. An element with 'position: fixed' is positioned relative to the browser viewport, meaning it stays in the exact same place even when the page is scrolled. Like absolute positioning, it is removed from the normal document flow."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":".navbar-fixed {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Sticky Positioning"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sticky positioning is a hybrid of relative and fixed positioning. An element is treated as 'position: relative' until it crosses a specified threshold distance from the viewport, at which point it becomes 'position: fixed'. It 'sticks' to that position as the user continues scrolling, typically bounded by its parent container."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":".header-sticky {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Positioning Rules"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static: Normal flow; offsets do not apply."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Relative: Normal flow space retained; offsets move it relative to itself."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Absolute: Removed from flow; positioned relative to nearest positioned ancestor."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fixed: Removed from flow; positioned relative to the viewport."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sticky: Toggles between relative and fixed based on scroll position."}]
